<section xml:id="HW1" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Homework 1</title>

  <p><alert> Due Thursday, December 29</alert></p>

  <introduction>
    <p> INSTRUCTIONS: Justify all statements. While you may ask for help and collaborate with others, the writing of the solutions needs to be in your own words, based on your own understanding.
    You may rely on standard results learned in the prerequisite class(es) you took and results that have been discussed in class or assigned for reading. Any other results should be used sparingly, if at all, and only if you can provide their proof. 
  </p>
  </introduction>

<exercises xml:id="hw1-ex">
    <exercise>
      <statement>
        <p>
        [From the book]: Chapter 11: 1.6a, 3.3ab, 3.9a, 3.12, 3.13
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>
 <exercise>
        <statement>
      <p>
    [Problem A]:  Let <m>R</m> be a ring and let <m>I=(a_1, \dots, a_n)</m> be an ideal of <m>R</m> generated by elements <m>a_1, \dots, a_n</m> of <m>R</m> . If <m>J</m> is an ideal of <m>R</m> , prove <m>I\subseteq J</m> if and only if <m>a_i\in J</m> for all <m>i</m> with <m>1\le i\le n</m> .
      </p>
</statement>
<solution>
 <p> This gives a concrete way of checking ideal containment. Use it in problem B below.</p> 
</solution>
    </exercise>
    <exercise>
      <statement>
        <p> [Problem B]:  Verify the following equalities of ideals in the polynomial ring <m>R=\mathbb Q[X,Y]</m> : 
<ol>
<li> <m>(X,Y)=(X+Y,X-Y)</m>   </li>
<li>  <m>(X,Y)=(X+XY,Y+XY,X^2,Y^2)</m> </li>
<li> <m>(2X^2+3Y^2-11, X^2-Y^2-3)=(X^2-4,Y^2-1) </m></li>
</ol>
  </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
 <p>This problem shows that the same ideal can have different generating sets and that different generating sets may have different numbers of elements. </p> 
</solution>
    </exercise>

    <exercise>
        <statement>
      <p>
    [Problem C]: Let <m>R</m> be a ring with <m>1\ne 0</m> and define <m>f\colon R\to R</m> by <m>f(r)=r^2</m> . Prove that <m>f</m> is a ring homomorphism if and only if <m>R</m> has characteristic <m>2</m> . 

  </p>
</statement>
    </exercise>



</exercises>
</section>